@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "COMMIT";
}

<div class="container-fluid py-4">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">

        <h1 class="fw-bold text-primary">
            COMMIT
        </h1>

        <p class="text-muted fs-5">
            Learn how the COMMIT statement permanently saves
            changes made during a SQL Server transaction.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         WHAT IS COMMIT?
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                What is COMMIT?
            </h2>

            <p>
                <strong>COMMIT</strong> is a SQL Server transaction
                command used to permanently save all changes made
                during the current transaction.
            </p>

            <p>
                Once a transaction is committed, the changes become
                permanent in the database and normally cannot be
                undone using ROLLBACK.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         WHY USE COMMIT?
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Why Do We Use COMMIT?
            </h2>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    To permanently save transaction changes.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    To confirm that all required operations
                    completed successfully.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    To make the transaction changes permanent.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    To complete a transaction successfully.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         BASIC SYNTAX
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                COMMIT Syntax
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRANSACTION;

-- SQL statements

COMMIT;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SIMPLE EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Simple COMMIT Example
            </h2>

            <p>
                Suppose we want to insert a new student into the
                Students table.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRANSACTION;

INSERT INTO Students
(
    StudentId,
    StudentName,
    City,
    Age
)
VALUES
(
    201,
    'Rahul',
    'Pune',
    22
);

COMMIT;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Here, the INSERT operation is performed inside the
                transaction. When <strong>COMMIT</strong> executes,
                the new student record is permanently saved.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         UPDATE WITH COMMIT
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                UPDATE with COMMIT
            </h2>

            <p>
                COMMIT can also be used after an UPDATE operation.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRANSACTION;

UPDATE Students
SET City = 'Mumbai'
WHERE StudentId = 201;

COMMIT;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The student's city is updated and the change is
                permanently saved after COMMIT.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         DELETE WITH COMMIT
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                DELETE with COMMIT
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRANSACTION;

DELETE FROM Students
WHERE StudentId = 201;

COMMIT;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The DELETE operation is performed inside the
                transaction and becomes permanent after COMMIT.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         MULTIPLE OPERATIONS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                COMMIT with Multiple Operations
            </h2>

            <p>
                A transaction can contain multiple SQL statements.
                COMMIT saves all of them together.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRANSACTION;

INSERT INTO Students
(
    StudentId,
    StudentName,
    City,
    Age
)
VALUES
(
    202,
    'Priya',
    'Pune',
    21
);

UPDATE Students
SET Age = 22
WHERE StudentId = 202;

COMMIT;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Both operations are part of the same transaction.
                When COMMIT executes, the changes are saved.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         COMMIT AND ROLLBACK
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                COMMIT vs ROLLBACK
            </h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-primary">

                        <tr>

                            <th>COMMIT</th>

                            <th>ROLLBACK</th>

                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>

                            <td>
                                Permanently saves changes.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Undoes uncommitted changes.
                            </td>

                        </tr>

                        <tr>

                            <td>
                                Completes the transaction successfully.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Cancels/undoes the transaction changes.
                            </td>

                        </tr>

                        <tr>

                            <td>
                                Used when the operation is successful.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Used when an error or unwanted result occurs.
                            </td>

                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         REAL WORLD EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Real-World Example
            </h2>

            <p>
                Consider an online shopping application.
            </p>

            <p>
                When a customer places an order, several operations
                may need to happen:
            </p>

            <ol>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Create the order.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Add order items.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Reduce product stock.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Save payment information.
                </li>

            </ol>

            <p>
                If all operations are successful, the application
                can COMMIT the transaction.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRANSACTION;

-- Create Order

-- Add Order Items

-- Update Stock

-- Save Payment

COMMIT;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Important Points
            </h2>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    COMMIT permanently saves transaction changes.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    COMMIT is normally used after successful
                    transaction operations.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    COMMIT completes the current transaction.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    After COMMIT, the transaction changes are no
                    longer available for normal ROLLBACK.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Multiple SQL statements can be committed together
                    as one transaction.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-primary shadow-sm mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Summary
        </h2>

        <p>
            <strong>COMMIT</strong> is used to permanently save
            changes made during a SQL Server transaction.
        </p>

        <p class="mb-0">
            A transaction should generally be committed when all
            required operations have completed successfully.
        </p>

    </div>


